d area","methodDirectNodeIds":{"2012_AMC_10B_Q02_m1":["cnm_p089","cnm_p188","cnm_p185","cnm_262"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p089","cnm_p188","cnm_p185","cnm_262","cnm_p088","cnm_p077","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b63eb07aae5525999a3eaa79"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (50 inches)","reason":"The center occupies 1 - 4(3/16) = 1/4 of the square. Its area is 100²/4 = 2500 square inches, and √2500 = 50 inches.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p088"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1995_AMC_8_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p088","cnm_p035","cnm_p080","cnm_p177"],"id":"remaining-area","label":"Find the area left for the center","methodDirectNodeIds":{"remaining-area":["cnm_p150","cnm_p089","cnm_p179","cnm_075"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_p089","cnm_p179","cnm_075","cnm_p088","cnm_p035","cnm_p080","cnm_p177"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b63eb07aae5525999a3eaa79"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, e=22/23","reason":"Let the large and small major semiaxes be A,a, with A>a>0. Equal eccentricity e gives minor/major ratio k=sqrt(1-e^2)>0 in both, so their standard equations show that they are uniformly similar with length ratio A/a. The general similar-plane-area theorem gives A/a=sqrt(2025)=45; no absolute ellipse-area formula is used. The foci order F-G-H puts the center separation at d=e(A+a)>0. To justify the common right vertex without trusting drawing scale, place the large center at 0 and the small at d on the x-axis. A small-boundary point is (d+a*u,k*a*v), with u^2+v^2=1. Substitution in the large ellipse gives normalized squared level(d^2+a^2+2*d*a*u)/A^2, whose maximum is (d+a)^2/A^2 at u=1. Containment and contact therefore force d+a=A; otherwise the smaller ellipse would either protrude or be strictly interior. Hence e(A+a)=A-a and e=(45-1)/(45+1)=22/23, D.","recordedAnswer":"D","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p088"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_054","cnm_042","cnm_p088","cnm_p185"],"id":"focal-sums-and-area-scaling","label":"Focal distance sums and area scaling","methodDirectNodeIds":{"focal-sums-and-area-scaling":["cnm_h183","cnm_h185","cnm_h186","math:similar-figure-area-scaling"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h183","cnm_h185","cnm_h186","math:similar-figure-area-scaling","cnm_054","cnm_042","cnm_p088","cnm_p185"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_p088"],"label":"3.MD.C.5a","layer":"US","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"code":"3.MD.C.5a","description":"A square with side length 1 unit, called “a unit square,” is said to have “one square unit” of area, and can be used to measure area.","id":"98","label":"3.MD.C.5a","metadata":{"clusterId":"26","clusterName":"Geometric Measurement: Understand Concepts Of Area And Relate Area To Multiplication And To Addition.","clusterOrdinal":"C","domainCode":"MD","domainId":"11","domainName":"Measurement And Data","ordinal":"5.a","sourceCode":"3.MD.C.5.a"},"parentSourceId":"97","placement":{"grade":"3","meaning":"Native Common Core grade"},"reviewStatus":"source_only"},"sourceNodeId":"98","status":"partial","total":58}
